Turn the client into the shell package nexora patches, with a launcher screen.

The APK: three things about this tree are not what nexora wants, two of them
megabytes of dead weight.

CMake's if(ANDROID) block stages ~26 MB of 3rdparty/cs16client-extras, yapb
configs and downloaded bot graphs into android/app/src/main/assets, and gradle
packages all of it. Nexora ships the game content as its own game-assets.zip
release asset and installs it into the game directory, so the APK carries a
second copy that nothing reads. Emptied, and the graph download moves into the
desktop branch so yapb_graph_dl.py stops reaching out to the network at
configure time on a build whose only job is producing a shell.

The externalNativeBuild block then builds the client, the menu and the server
and packages every one of them: libclient is 6.5 MB and libmenu 6.3 MB per ABI.
Nexora injects its own build of both into this package after download (the
patch log shows "+ libclient_android_arm64.so"). The engine looks for the menu
as cl_dlls/libmenu_android_<arch>.so, then <gamelibdir>/libmenu_android_<arch>.so,
then libmenu.so from its own package - never a copy inside the client APK - and
the client dll is dlopen'ed out of the injected set. yapb and ReGameDLL_CS land
in the build graph but in neither the APK nor nexora's Android bundle.

The launcher: upstream went straight from onCreate to XashActivity, so there
was nowhere to change a parameter. MainActivity now shows a terminal-style
screen - argv field, a Launch button, and the parameters remembered between
sessions. The su.xash.engine.MOD intent still goes straight through, because
something already asked the engine to run this package and a dialog in the way
would be wrong.

-dll @metamod replaces the -dll @yapb upstream passes, because the game package
nexora builds has no yapb bootstrap: the engine is pointed at metamod and the
bot plugin rides along as a metamod plugin. It is appended by the launcher and
shown on its own row called "GameDLL Loader" rather than in the argv field, so
it is visible without being editable. Typing your own -dll means two loaders
competing for the same dll list, so that disables the button and explains why
instead of silently dropping one of them.

Also: only the arm ABIs, since an x86 install never got a cl_dll from anywhere;
useLegacyPackaging, because the injected .so files are dlopen'ed straight out of
the APK; roundIcon pointed at the round variant; and minSdk 21 -> 24, matching
the API level nexora injects against, so the APK does not install on a device
that cannot load what gets injected into it.

android/ci/build-amxx.sh builds the real libclient/libmenu for the bundles, from
this same pinned tree.

Applied by the "Build the client APK" step, so it is tied to the commit in
cs16-client.pin rather than to a fork of it. A file this size does not belong
in a fork of upstream that has to be re-applied on every bump - if cs16-client
keeps this patch alive for more than a couple of releases, the next step is a
berkchy fork and a pin that points at it.

